Blocking - wait till task is completed without doing anything
Non blocking  - continue doing other tasks while waiting for the task to be completed

Think of blocking vs non-blocking like ordering food at a restaurant:
Blocking → You place an order and just stand there, waiting until the chef finishes cooking. You can’t do anything else.
Non-blocking → You place an order, get a ticket, and go do other things. The waiter notifies you when the food is ready.

In spring, we can implement non-blocking using:
1. Thread Based Non Blocking - Asynchronous programming with @Async annotation - method will run in separate thread
2. Event Based Non Blocking - Reactive programming with Project Reactor (Mono, Flux)

Where to use:
1. Logging
2. Sending email
3. Calling external APIs
4. File upload

✅ In summary:
A non-blocking task lets the system continue doing useful work instead of waiting idly.
This improves scalability, responsiveness, and resource efficiency, especially in web applications that handle many concurrent users.

// Example of blocking code
public class BlockingExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Task 1 started");
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000); // Simulate a blocking operation
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        System.out.println("Task 1 completed");
        System.out.println("Task 2 started");
        System.out.println("Task 2 completed");
    }
}
// In this example, Task 2 waits for Task 1 to complete before starting.

// Example of non-blocking code using @Async in Spring
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableAsync;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.concurrent.Executor;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableAsync
public class NonBlockingExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(NonBlockingExample.class, args);
        MyService service = new MyService();
        service.task1();
        service.task2();
    }
    @Bean
    public Executor taskExecutor() {
        return Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
    }
}
@Service
class MyService {
    @Async
    public CompletableFuture<Void> task1() {
        System.out.println("Task 1 started");
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000); // Simulate a long-running task
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        System.out.println("Task 1 completed");
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(null);
    }
    public void task2() {
        System.out.println("Task 2 started");
        System.out.println("Task 2 completed");
    }
}
// In this example, Task 2 starts immediately without waiting for Task 1 to complete.


